„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFTherefore whether it were I or they, so we preach, and so ye believed.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses˜„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwNow if Christ be preached that he rose from the dead, how say some among you that there is no resurrection of the dead?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFBut if there be no resurrection of the dead, then is Christ not risen:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTand if Christ be not risen, then is our preaching vain, and your faith is also vain.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ Yea, and we are found false witnesses of God; because we have testified of God that he raised up Christ: whom he raised not up, if so be that the dead rise not.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ4For if the dead rise not, then is not Christ raised:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIand if Christ be not raised, your faith is vain; ye are yet in your sins.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ>Then they also which are fallen asleep in Christ are perished.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNIf in this life only we have hope in Christ, we are of all men most miserable.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses™„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙUBut now is Christ risen from the dead, and become the firstfruits of them that slept.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙKFor since by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBFor as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgBut every man in his own order: Christ the firstfruits; afterward they that are Christ's at his coming.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ›Then cometh the end, when he shall have delivered up the kingdom to God, even the Father; when he shall have put down all rule and all authority and power.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ?For he must reign, till he hath put all enemies under his feet.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ0The last enemy that shall be destroyed is death.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢For he hath put all things under his feet. But when he saith all things are put under him, it is manifest that he is excepted, which did put all things under him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd when all things shall be subdued unto him, then shall the Son also himself be subject unto him that put all things under him, that God may be all in all.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~Else what shall they do which are baptized for the dead, if the dead rise not at all? why are they then baptized for the dead?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ(And why stand we in jeopardy every hour?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOI protest by your rejoicing which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord, I die daily.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙšIf after the manner of men I have fought with beasts at Ephesus, what advantageth it me, if the dead rise not? let us eat and drink; for to morrow we die.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book-§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse"¦verses’„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ:Be not deceived: evil communications corrupt good manners.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhAwake to righteousness, and sin not; for some have not the knowledge of God: I speak this to your shame.¦layout ªlast_verse"…¤book-§chapter«first_verse#ªlast_verse&¦verses”„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSBut some man will say, How are the dead raised up? and with what body do they come?¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBThou fool, that which thou sowest is not quickened, except it die:¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ„and that which thou sowest, thou sowest not that body that shall be, but bare grain, it may chance of wheat, or of some other grain:¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPbut God giveth it a body as it hath pleased him, and to every seed his own body.¦layout ªlast_verse&…¤book-§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse)¦verses“„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‰All flesh is not the same flesh: but there is one kind of flesh of men, another flesh of beasts, another of fishes, and another of birds.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŒThere are also celestial bodies, and bodies terrestrial: but the glory of the celestial is one, and the glory of the terrestrial is another.¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙThere is one glory of the sun, and another glory of the moon, and another glory of the stars: for one star differeth from another star in glory.¦layout ªlast_verse)…¤book-§chapter«first_verse*ªlast_verse1¦verses˜„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`So also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in corruption; it is raised in incorruption:¦layout ªlast_verse*„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^it is sown in dishonour; it is raised in glory: it is sown in weakness; it is raised in power:¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙqit is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body. There is a natural body, and there is a spiritual body.¦layout ªlast_verse,„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙlAnd so it is written, The first man Adam was made a living soul; the last Adam was made a quickening spirit.¦layout ªlast_verse-„¥verse.¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpHowbeit that was not first which is spiritual, but that which is natural; and afterward that which is spiritual.¦layout ªlast_verse.„¥verse/¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNThe first man is of the earth, earthy: the second man is the Lord from heaven.¦layout ªlast_verse/„¥verse0¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsAs is the earthy, such are they also that are earthy: and as is the heavenly, such are they also that are heavenly.¦layout ªlast_verse0„¥verse1¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ[And as we have borne the image of the earthy, we shall also bear the image of the heavenly.¦layout ªlast_verse1…¤book-§chapter«first_verse2ªlast_verse9¦verses˜„¥verse2¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNow this I say, brethren, that flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God; neither doth corruption inherit incorruption.¦layout ªlast_verse2„¥verse3¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRBehold, I shew you a mystery; We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed,¦layout ªlast_verse3„¥verse4¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙin a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trump: for the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised incorruptible, and we shall be changed.¦layout ªlast_verse4„¥verse5¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWFor this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality.¦layout ªlast_verse5„¥verse6¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÃSo when this corruptible shall have put on incorruption, and this mortal shall have put on immortality, then shall be brought to pass the saying that is written, Death is swallowed up in victory.¦layout ªlast_verse6„¥verse7¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ;O death, where is thy sting? O grave, where is thy victory?¦layout ªlast_verse7„¥verse8¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ>The sting of death is sin; and the strength of sin is the law.¦layout ªlast_verse8„¥verse9¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPBut thanks be to God, which giveth us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.¦layout ªlast_verse9…¤book-§chapter«first_verse:ªlast_verse:¦verses‘„¥verse:¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye stedfast, unmoveable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, forasmuch as ye know that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.¦layout ªlast_verse: